A||||4||DiggityDan||Overheating V6||||1||
Z||000000||DiggityDan||05-21-2000||04:00 AM||||Well, I got my O2 sensor in and code 13 went away, last time i drove the car, code 32 was no where in sight - we'll see if it stays that way. Really impressed with that $22.00 O2 sensor, mph increased about 6.5 miles. <BR>Ok, next question, I bought a thermostat for my car and installed it this morning, took it out and the temperature immediately started climing. The problem is that it tried to overheat on me. When I was idling it was fine, but driving it would climb really fast. I know the fan is working properly and I know the thermostat isn't stuck. I can only imagine it being a clogged radiator or the impellers on the water pump are trashed. Anybody had same problem, what did you finally do to fix it. I know the bearings in the water pump are good and its not leaking.<BR>Any help would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-21-2000||05:27 AM||||You KNOW it isn't stuck.  The thermostat has been boiled and actually seen while open?<P>Big vapor lock?<BR> <BR>Rotors do loosen on shafts, a slipping impeller would not necessarily keep up with a hard running engine.<P>Demons. Hot little demons from the nether world beneath the great lakes area.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Heisenhund||05-21-2000||10:41 PM||||I've got the same problem...kinda. When I start the car, and begin driving, the temp slowly creeps up to normal. Through normal. Into the very red area of the gage. At first I panicked, having lost a Fiero engine to this problem about 14 years ago. New stat (180F), change coolant, burp it real good (repeatedly). No change. After it makes a trip into the deadly red zone, the stat "appears" to open, temp drops to an acceptable level & stays there. Externally the water pump looks good and there's no bearing noise. There is a bit of belt squeal on startup, but that goes away in 10 secs of idling @ 1000 RPM.<P>I'm beginning to think the internals of the water pump are trashed on mine too. Unfortunately, new part money doesn't become available until the 1st. If you replace yours (or figure out the "real" problem) before I do, post the solution. I will, of course do likewise.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Guinea Pig||05-21-2000||11:07 PM||||A question to your question.  Is the air dam under the front on your car?  Frequently if this is gone or severely damaged it will not direct enough air to the radiator to keep temps down when car is moving.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DiggityDan||05-22-2000||02:53 AM||||I know for fact that it is not stuck, I know it opens, I don't know at what exact temperature it opens, haven't checked that yet. I know the spring seems a bit stiffer than the one I found in the trunk when I bought the car. And they are both the same temp.<BR>Also, my air dam is completely intac, absolutely no body damage on my car. I've recently discovered I have a ticking in my car. I have a feeling its either a wristpin or a bearing so I know I have to decide if I want to just rebuild it or upgrade to the 3800 supercharger - needless to say I am leaning to the 3800. When I figure out the overheating problem I'll let everyone know.||||reg||1
